  "sources": [
    {
      "id": "rusqlite_readme",
      "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rusqlite/rusqlite/master/README.md",
      "bytes": 16177,
      "sha256": "267fd07f013a6cdcd230a6b9b7ed21df1588cc156f0f0bb429d7957274cf3441",
      "status": "read",
      "relevant_lines": [
        {
          "line": 21,
          "text": "# `bundled` causes us to automatically compile and link in an up to date"
        },
        {
          "line": 30,
          "text": "rusqlite = { version = \"0.40.1\", features = [\"bundled\"] }"
        },
        {
          "line": 96,
          "text": "* [`backup`](https://docs.rs/rusqlite/~0/rusqlite/backup/index.html)"
        },
        {
          "line": 97,
          "text": "  allows use of SQLite's online backup API."
        },
        {
          "line": 122,
          "text": "* `bundled` uses a bundled version of SQLite.  This is a good option for cases where linking to SQLite is complicated, such as Windows."
        },
        {
          "line": 123,
          "text": "* `sqlcipher` looks for the SQLCipher library to link against instead of SQLite. This feature overrides `bundled`."
        },
        {
          "line": 124,
          "text": "* `bundled-sqlcipher` uses a bundled version of SQLCipher. This searches for and links against a system-installed crypto library to provide the crypto implementation."
        },
        {
          "line": 125,
          "text": "* `bundled-sqlcipher-vendored-openssl` allows using bundled-sqlcipher with a vendored version of OpenSSL (via the `openssl-sys` crate) as the crypto provider."
        },
        {
          "line": 126,
          "text": "  - As the name implies this depends on the `bundled-sqlcipher` feature, and automatically turns it on."
        },
        {
          "line": 159,
          "text": "* If you use the `bundled`, `bundled-sqlcipher`, or `bundled-sqlcipher-vendored-openssl` features, `libsqlite3-sys` will use the"
        },
        {
          "line": 167,
          "text": "  features = [\"bundled\"]"
        },
        {
          "line": 169,
          "text": "* When using any of the `bundled` features, the build script will honor `SQLITE_MAX_VARIABLE_NUMBER` and `SQLITE_MAX_EXPR_DEPTH` variables. It will also honor a `LIBSQLITE3_FLAGS` variable, which can have a format like `\"-USQLITE_ALPHA -DSQLITE_BETA SQLITE_GAMMA ...\"`. That would disable the `SQLITE_ALPHA` flag, and set the `SQLITE_BETA` and `SQLIT"
        },
        {
          "line": 170,
          "text": "* When using `bundled-sqlcipher` (and not also using `bundled-sqlcipher-vendored-openssl`), `libsqlite3-sys` will need to"
        },
        {
          "line": 173,
          "text": "* When linking against a SQLite (or SQLCipher) library already on the system (so *not* using any of the `bundled` features), you can set the `SQLITE3_LIB_DIR` (or `SQLCIPHER_LIB_DIR`) environment variable to point to a directory containing the library. You can also set the `SQLITE3_INCLUDE_DIR` (or `SQLCIPHER_INCLUDE_DIR`) variable to point to the "
        },
        {
          "line": 206,
          "text": "If you use any of the `bundled` features, you will get pregenerated bindings for the"
        },
        {
          "line": 207,
          "text": "bundled version of SQLite/SQLCipher. If you need other specific pregenerated binding"
        },
        {
          "line": 212,
          "text": "included with the bundled build. You generally should have `buildtime_bindgen`"
        },
        {
          "line": 214,
          "text": "SQLite you link with in sync with what rusqlite would have bundled, (usually the"
        },
        {
          "line": 223,
          "text": "`--features bundled`, and `--features \"bundled-full session buildtime_bindgen\"`."
        },
        {
          "line": 225,
          "text": "If running bindgen is problematic for you, `--features bundled-full` enables"
        },
        {
          "line": 226,
          "text": "bundled and all features which don't require binding generation, and can be used"
        },
        {
          "line": 232,
          "text": "- Ensure `cargo clippy --workspace --features bundled` passes without warnings."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sqlx_readme",
      "url": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/launchbadge/sqlx/main/README.md",
      "bytes": 18824,
      "sha256": "0a0e168f0825a07501f4db653090c3cf71c7eceebf23f1e8efa56c5e9676e367",
      "status": "read",
      "relevant_lines": [
        {
          "line": 68,
          "text": "SQLx is an async, pure Rust<sub>†</sub> SQL crate featuring compile-time checked queries without a DSL."
        },
        {
          "line": 70,
          "text": "-   **Truly Asynchronous**. Built from the ground-up using async/await for maximum concurrency."
        },
        {
          "line": 72,
          "text": "-   **Compile-time checked queries** (if you want). See [SQLx is not an ORM](#sqlx-is-not-an-orm)."
        },
        {
          "line": 74,
          "text": "-   **Database Agnostic**. Support for [PostgreSQL], [MySQL], [MariaDB], [SQLite]."
        },
        {
          "line": 79,
          "text": "-   **Runtime Agnostic**. Works on different runtimes ([`async-std`] / [`tokio`] / [`actix`]) and TLS backends ([`native-tls`], [`rustls`])."
        },
        {
          "line": 83,
          "text": "† The SQLite driver uses the libsqlite3 C library as SQLite is an embedded database (the only way"
        },
        {
          "line": 84,
          "text": "we could be pure Rust for SQLite is by porting _all_ of SQLite to Rust)."
        },
        {
          "line": 86,
          "text": "†† SQLx uses `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` unless the `sqlite` feature is enabled."
        },
        {
          "line": 87,
          "text": "The SQLite driver directly invokes the SQLite3 API via `libsqlite3-sys`, which requires `unsafe`."
        },
        {
          "line": 92,
          "text": "[sqlite]: https://sqlite.org/"
        },
        {
          "line": 100,
          "text": "-   Cross-platform. Being native Rust, SQLx will compile anywhere Rust is supported."
        },
        {
          "line": 104,
          "text": "-   Row streaming. Data is read asynchronously from the database and decoded on demand."
        },
        {
          "line": 114,
          "text": "-   Asynchronous notifications using `LISTEN` and `NOTIFY` for [PostgreSQL]."
        },
        {
          "line": 122,
          "text": "SQLx is compatible with the [`async-std`], [`tokio`], and [`actix`] runtimes; and, the [`native-tls`] and [`rustls`] TLS backends. When adding the dependency, you must choose a runtime feature that is `runtime` + `tls`."
        },
        {
          "line": 124,
          "text": "[`async-std`]: https://github.com/async-rs/async-std"
        },
        {
          "line": 146,
          "text": "# async-std (no TLS)"
        },
        {
          "line": 147,
          "text": "sqlx = { version = \"0.9\", features = [ \"runtime-async-std\" ] }"
        },
        {
          "line": 148,
          "text": "# async-std + native-tls"
        },
        {
          "line": 149,
          "text": "sqlx = { version = \"0.9\", features = [ \"runtime-async-std\", \"tls-native-tls\" ] }"
        },
        {
          "line": 150,
          "text": "# async-std + rustls with ring and WebPKI CA certificates"
        },
        {
          "line": 151,
          "text": "sqlx = { version = \"0.9\", features = [ \"runtime-async-std\", \"tls-rustls-ring-webpki\" ] }"
        },
        {
          "line": 152,
          "text": "# async-std + rustls with ring and platform's native CA certificates"
        }
      ]
    },
